WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.829 --> 00:00:03.603
The program describes the part, not the tool.

2
00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:07.606
The control adds the tool from tables, the offsets.

3
00:00:08.301 --> 00:00:08.963
Three matter.

4
00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:15.311
The work offset, G54 to G59, says where the part zero sits.

5
00:00:16.331 --> 00:00:20.511
Tool length compensation uses G43 with an H number.

6
00:00:21.247 --> 00:00:24.370
It adds how far each tool sticks out of the spindle.

7
00:00:25.385 --> 00:00:31.144
Cutter radius compensation uses G41 left of the contour and G42 right,

8
00:00:31.271 --> 00:00:32.177
with a D number.

9
00:00:32.913 --> 00:00:35.978
It shifts the path sideways by the tool radius.

10
00:00:36.972 --> 00:00:43.183
G49 and G40 switch the length compensation and the radius compensation off again.

11
00:00:44.481 --> 00:00:45.445
Why this split.

12
00:00:46.146 --> 00:00:48.932
The programmer writes the contour of the part once.

13
00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:57.697
If a new 10 mm end mill measures 9.98 mm, the setter enters radius 4.99.

14
00:00:58.386 --> 00:01:01.103
The same program still makes the right size.

15
00:01:02.114 --> 00:01:05.225
On top of each measured value sits a wear offset.

16
00:01:06.008 --> 00:01:09.979
That is a small correction the operator enters when sizes drift.

17
00:01:11.021 --> 00:01:14.992
A bore measures 0.01 mm small on diameter?

18
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:21.295
Then the operator changes the radius by 0.005 mm.

19
00:01:22.043 --> 00:01:26.153
That is half the error, because a radius counts twice in a diameter.

20
00:01:27.499 --> 00:01:31.574
A crash is the tool or spindle hitting the part, the fixture or the machine.

21
00:01:32.582 --> 00:01:33.638
Typical causes.

22
00:01:34.414 --> 00:01:41.752
A wrong number typed in, such as 121.43 becoming 112.43.

23
00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:46.553
The wrong H number, so tool 5 with the length of tool 3.

24
00:01:47.529 --> 00:01:49.456
The wrong work offset is active.

25
00:01:50.162 --> 00:01:54.283
That is G55 while the part sits on G54.

26
00:01:55.314 --> 00:01:58.031
A tool sits in the wrong pocket of the magazine.

27
00:01:58.743 --> 00:02:02.063
Or there is an incremental move where absolute was meant.

28
00:02:03.106 --> 00:02:06.473
The common thread is a table that does not match reality.

29
00:02:07.142 --> 00:02:09.510
The program itself is usually fine.

30
00:02:10.844 --> 00:02:14.513
The process engineer decides which numbers are typed by hand,

31
00:02:14.722 --> 00:02:15.767
and which are measured.

32
00:02:16.783 --> 00:02:19.918
A presetter is a measuring stand next to the machine.

33
00:02:20.655 --> 00:02:23.766
It sends tool lengths to the control electronically.

34
00:02:24.762 --> 00:02:28.535
A touch probe in the spindle sets G54 itself.

35
00:02:29.555 --> 00:02:33.653
A tool setter in the machine checks the length after every tool change.

36
00:02:34.662 --> 00:02:37.715
Each automatic step removes a chance to mistype.

37
00:02:38.765 --> 00:02:42.062
The question for the engineer is which crashes we had.

38
00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:44.978
And which step would have stopped each one?

39
00:02:46.339 --> 00:02:48.464
A cell had 6 crashes last year.

40
00:02:49.173 --> 00:02:50.868
The causes are in the reports.

41
00:02:51.862 --> 00:02:54.521
Three times a tool length was typed in wrong.

42
00:02:55.277 --> 00:02:57.309
Twice there was a wrong H number.

43
00:02:58.035 --> 00:02:59.951
Once there was a wrong work offset.

44
00:03:01.022 --> 00:03:06.479
Each crash cost on average 12,000 euros in repairs and lost machine hours.

45
00:03:07.228 --> 00:03:10.421
That is the shop’s own figure, stated as an assumption.

46
00:03:11.519 --> 00:03:16.186
The proposal is a presetter that sends tool data to the control electronically.

47
00:03:16.884 --> 00:03:20.785
An assumed 25,000 euros, including the link.

48
00:03:21.818 --> 00:03:25.011
It removes typed lengths and H-number mix-ups.

49
00:03:25.718 --> 00:03:27.669
It does nothing for the work offset.

50
00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:32.004
Which crashes would it have prevented, what does that save a year,

51
00:03:32.259 --> 00:03:33.687
and how long is the payback?

52
00:03:35.035 --> 00:03:37.740
3 plus 2 is 5 of the 6 crashes.

53
00:03:38.448 --> 00:03:42.848
5 times 12,000 euros is 60,000 euros a year.

54
00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:47.879
The payback is 25,000 divided by 60,000.

55
00:03:48.656 --> 00:03:52.163
That is 0.42 years, about 5 months.

56
00:03:53.161 --> 00:03:57.109
The sixth crash, the wrong work offset, needs another fix.

57
00:03:57.813 --> 00:03:59.949
Set the part zero with a touch probe.

58
00:04:00.685 --> 00:04:03.425
Or build in a check at the start of the program

59
00:04:03.495 --> 00:04:05.840
that stops when the wrong offset is active.

60
00:04:06.844 --> 00:04:09.305
Also ask whether last year was typical.

61
00:04:10.017 --> 00:04:12.432
Six crashes in one cell is a lot.
